# Cupcake WiFi Configuration
#
# RPi     : copy as  /boot/firmware/cupcake-wifi.txt  onto the SD card boot
#           partition before inserting it into the device.
#
# x86 PC  : copy as  cupcake-wifi.txt  onto a USB stick labelled CUPCAKECFG, or
#           onto the EFI partition, together with any certificate files it names.
#
# x86 VM  : WiFi is rarely needed on VMs (they use virtual NICs).
#           Preferred: use the admin UI at /admin/wifi after first boot.
#           Alternative: SSH in, write /opt/cupcake/wifi-config.txt, then
#           reboot (or run  sudo /opt/cupcake/first-boot.sh  directly).
#
# The file is read on every boot but only acted on when present. It is deleted,
# together with any certificate files it names, once the connection is set up,
# so no password or key is left on the card or stick.
#
# Use ONE of the two templates below: keep its lines, delete or comment out the
# other.
#
# Write each value exactly as it is, after the "=": no quotes or brackets. Values
# may contain "=", "#" and spaces, and files saved in Windows Notepad work too.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Two-letter country code, for the radio's legal channel set (RPi).
COUNTRY=GB

# Wireless interface. Leave commented to use the first wireless interface.
#INTERFACE=wlan0


# ── Template 1: WPA2-Personal (home or lab router with one shared password) ──

SSID=MyLabNetwork
AUTH_TYPE=wpa2-personal
PASSWORD=MySecretPassphrase


# ── Template 2: WPA2-Enterprise / eduroam (university or company network) ────
#
# You sign in with your own username and password instead of a shared one.
# These five lines are enough to connect to eduroam at most universities
# (PEAP with MSCHAPv2):
#
#SSID=eduroam
#AUTH_TYPE=wpa2-enterprise
#EAP_METHOD=peap
#IDENTITY=ab123@university.ac.uk
#PASSWORD=YourUniversityPassword
#
# Optional settings. Your IT department, or the eduroam Configuration Assistant
# Tool (https://cat.eduroam.org), lists the right values for your institution.
#
# Inner method for PEAP or TTLS: mschapv2 | pap | chap (TTLS often uses pap).
#PHASE2_AUTH=mschapv2
#
# Outer identity sent before the encrypted tunnel; keeps your username private.
#ANONYMOUS_IDENTITY=anonymous@university.ac.uk
#
# Server verification (recommended, not required). Without it the device does
# not check that it is talking to your university's server, so a fake access
# point named "eduroam" could collect the password. Either:
#
#   trust the operating system's certificate store and check the server name
#   (Raspberry Pi; no file needed):
#CA_CERT=system
#SERVER_DOMAIN=radius.university.ac.uk
#
#   or name a CA certificate file from your IT department, copied next to this
#   file (Raspberry Pi and x86):
#CA_CERT=eduroam-ca.pem
#
# EAP-TLS only (sign in with a certificate instead of a password). Copy the
# files next to this one.
#EAP_METHOD=tls
#CLIENT_CERT=client.pem
#CLIENT_KEY=client.key.pem
#CLIENT_KEY_PASSWORD=KeyPasswordIfAny
